@h1 The mental model h1@

Wit is a stack of layers. Each one is optional — you only climb as high as your
document needs, and most documents stay near the bottom.

@h2 Layer 0 — Prose h2@

Plain paragraphs separated by blank lines, with @strong bold strong@ and
@em italics em@ inline. Most of any document lives here, and this layer alone is
a complete, valid document.

@h2 Layer 1 — Nodes h2@

Wrap content in a named structure with a node. The built-in vocabulary —
headings, lists, tables, figures, sectioning — needs no setup; you just use it.

@h2 Layer 2 — Definitions h2@

Name a reusable piece once — a callout, a figure, a chapter — and use it
anywhere. These are your own components; change one in a single place.

@h2 Layer 3 — Data h2@

Records and collections hold structured values you can pull into prose or hand
to a table, instead of typing the same fact in five spots.

@h2 Layer 4 — Logic h2@

Conditionals and iteration build content @em from em@ that data — a draft
versus a final, a table generated from a list.

@h2 The governing rule h2@

Content goes in a node's @strong body strong@; parameters are @strong metadata strong@.
Structure appears only where the author reaches for it — never by accident.
